// heartsim tutorial: the standard echo views and how to find them. Original text by the heartsim authors,
// MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-core/src/tutorial.rs.
// Every probe position, marker direction and manoeuvre follows Mitchell et al. 2019 (see references);
// the targets and tolerances behind "view found" are designed (heartsim_echo::views) and have not been
// reviewed by a sonographer. This teaches orientation and view recognition in the model, not hand skill.
Tutorial(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "13_echo_views",
    title: "Echo: the standard views",
    summary: "The four windows and the six probe movements, then thirteen standard views in the order the guideline teaches them: long axis, the two right-heart views, the four short-axis levels, the apical four-chamber and its neighbours, and the subcostal four-chamber. Each page asks you to find the view with the probe. Three last pages measure on the model: LV diameters on an M-mode, TAPSE, and tissue Doppler at the annulus.",
    references: [
        "Mitchell C, Rahko PS, Blauwet LA, et al. Guidelines for performing a comprehensive transthoracic echocardiographic examination in adults: recommendations from the American Society of Echocardiography. J Am Soc Echocardiogr 2019;32:1-64. doi:10.1016/j.echo.2018.06.004 (probe movements p5; PLAX, RV outflow and inflow p23-25; short-axis views p25-26; apical views p26 and p30; subcostal p31; M-mode p18; LV dimensions, left atrium p31; aortic root p32; TAPSE p33; tissue Doppler p44).",
    ],
    pages: [
        Page(
            title: "Windows and movements",
            markdown: r#"
The heart is imaged through a few **windows** where bone and lung leave a gap: beside the sternum
(**parasternal**), over the apex (**apical**), under the breastbone (**subcostal**) and above it
(**suprasternal**). The four buttons on the Echo page start the probe at each.

Six words describe what the hand does (Mitchell et al., p5):

* **slide** the transducer over the skin;
* **rotate** it, turning the index marker about the beam;
* **tilt** it, so the beam sweeps to another plane;
* **rock** it, swinging within the same plane toward or away from the marker;
* **angle** the beam from the same spot toward a structure;
* and the machine's **depth**.

This model has a **supine** patient. The guideline images the parasternal and apical windows in the
left lateral position, so some views are harder here, and a few cannot be reached at all (the last
page says which).
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Parasternal)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "You keep the transducer where it is and aim the beam toward the aortic valve. Which movement is that?",
                choices: ["Angle", "Slide", "Rotate", "Depth"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Angling keeps the position and points the beam at a structure. Sliding changes the skin position, rotating turns the marker, and depth is a setting of the machine.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "The index marker",
            markdown: r#"
The transducer has an **index marker**, an orange dot on the probe and on the sector. Whatever
lies on the marker side of the beam is drawn on the **right** of the image (the usual adult
display). In the apical views the left heart appears on the right of the screen, because the
marker points toward the patient's left side or toward the bed (Mitchell et al., p26).

On the Echo page the marker's position is also given as a clock face on the chest: 12 o'clock
toward the head, 3 o'clock toward the patient's left. Rotating changes it.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Apical)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "A structure lies on the marker side of the beam. Where is it drawn?",
                choices: ["On the right of the image", "On the left of the image", "At the top", "It depends on the depth"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The marker side is the right of the sector, so the marker side is where the image's right is.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Parasternal long axis",
            markdown: r#"
Place the probe at the **third or fourth intercostal space** beside the left sternal border, the
marker toward the **right shoulder** (about 9 to 10 o'clock). The ventricle should lie across the
sector; **the apex should not be seen**, and a short, rounded "false apex" is cured by rotating,
tilting or angling (Mitchell et al., p23-24). You should see two of the three aortic leaflets and
both mitral leaflets, and no papillary muscle.

Find the view. The page tells you what to change; **Show me** plays the movement.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Parasternal)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("plax")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "plax")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "In the parasternal long axis the apex of the left ventricle is normally:",
                choices: ["Not in the image", "At the top centre of the sector", "On the marker side, deep", "Replaced by the right atrium"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "In most patients the apex is not seen in this view. If the ventricle looks short and rounded, that is a false apex: rotate, tilt or angle to fix it.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Right ventricular outflow",
            markdown: r#"
From the long axis, **tilt the beam anteriorly** and **rotate slightly clockwise** (Mitchell et al.,
p25). The right ventricular outflow tract opens into the pulmonary valve and the main pulmonary
artery.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Parasternal)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("rv_outflow")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "rv_outflow")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which structure does the right ventricular outflow view lead you to?",
                choices: ["The pulmonary valve and main pulmonary artery", "The aortic valve and ascending aorta", "The coronary sinus", "The inferior vena cava"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The outflow tract of the right ventricle ends at the pulmonary valve, with the main pulmonary artery beyond it.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Right ventricular inflow",
            markdown: r#"
**Tilt toward the patient's right hip**, with a little counterclockwise rotation if needed
(Mitchell et al., p25). The tricuspid valve lies in the centre of the sector, the right ventricle in
the upper part and the right atrium in the lower part.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Parasternal)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("rv_inflow")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "rv_inflow")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "In the right ventricular inflow view the valve in the centre of the sector is:",
                choices: ["The tricuspid valve", "The mitral valve", "The aortic valve", "The pulmonary valve"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Inflow to the right ventricle crosses the tricuspid valve: it sits between the right atrium below and the right ventricle above.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Short axis: the great vessels and aortic valve",
            markdown: r#"
From the long axis **rotate the probe 90 degrees clockwise**, so that the beam cuts across the left
ventricle instead of along it (Mitchell et al., p25). At the level of the great vessels the aorta is
seen in cross-section with its cusps, the right ventricular outflow in front of it and the left atrium
behind. Tilt toward the base of the heart to reach this level; one interspace higher often helps.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Parasternal)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("psax_av")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "psax_av")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "What does the aortic valve look like in the short-axis view at this level?",
                choices: ["A round valve in cross-section with its cusps", "A long tube with two leaflets", "A four-chamber outline", "A line along the septum"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The short-axis plane cuts across the aortic root, so the valve is a round profile with three cusps; in the long axis it is a tube.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Short axis: the mitral valve",
            markdown: r#"
**Tilt toward the apex**, inferiorly and slightly leftward, stopping at the mitral valve (Mitchell et al., p26).
The left ventricle is cut across, and the right ventricle is the crescent at the top and left of the
sector. The leaflets should open and close fully.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Parasternal)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("psax_mv")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "psax_mv")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "In the short axis at the mitral valve, the right ventricle appears as:",
                choices: ["A crescent around the top and left of the left ventricle", "A circle in the centre", "A tube below the left atrium", "It is not in the plane"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The right ventricle wraps around the round left ventricle, so in this cut it is a crescent at the top and left.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Short axis: the papillary muscles",
            markdown: r#"
Tilt a little further, to just below the tips of the mitral leaflets, at the level of the papillary
muscles (Mitchell et al., p26). The ventricle should look **circular**, with the two papillary
muscles as round bodies in the cavity, and the wall is cut evenly all round. This is the level for
judging global and regional wall motion.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Parasternal)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("psax_papillary")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "psax_papillary")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "What shows that the plane is at the papillary level?",
                choices: ["Two round muscle bodies inside a circular cavity", "The mitral leaflets across the cavity", "The aortic cusps", "A tapered apex"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Just below the leaflet tips the cavity is round and holds the anterolateral and posteromedial papillary muscles.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Short axis: the apex",
            markdown: r#"
For the last short-axis level **tilt or slide one or two interspaces down and toward the left** (Mitchell
et al., p26). Only the tip of the left ventricle is cut: a small circle of cavity inside a thick
wall, with no valve leaflet and, usually, no right ventricle.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Parasternal)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("psax_apex")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "psax_apex")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "At the apical level the short-axis view shows:",
                choices: ["A small cavity with no valve leaflets", "The mitral valve", "The aortic valve", "Both atria"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Near the apex there are no valves: only a small cavity surrounded by wall.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Apical four-chamber",
            markdown: r#"
Put the probe at the **apical impulse**, near the fifth intercostal space and the mid-axillary
line, with the marker **toward the bed** (Mitchell et al., p26). All four chambers are shown, the
**apex of the left ventricle at the top and centre**, left-sided structures on the right of the
image. The left ventricle should fill about two thirds of the long axis and taper to a point; a
round apex means the view is **foreshortened**.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Apical)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("a4c")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "a4c")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "The ventricle looks short and its apex is round. What does that suggest?",
                choices: ["The view is foreshortened", "The ventricle is hypertrophied", "The marker is on the wrong side", "The depth is too small"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "A rounded apex means the plane misses the true apex: slide toward the apex or tilt until the ventricle tapers.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Right-ventricle-focused four-chamber",
            markdown: r#"
From the four-chamber view, **rotate slightly counterclockwise** while keeping the probe at the
apex, to open the right ventricle as wide as possible, and keep the plane through the centre of
the left ventricle (Mitchell et al., p26). It is the view for right-ventricular size and
tricuspid annular motion.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Apical)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("a4c_rv")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "a4c_rv")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Compared with the four-chamber view, the right-ventricle-focused view is made by:",
                choices: ["A slight counterclockwise rotation", "A 90-degree clockwise rotation", "Tilting the beam posteriorly", "Moving to the subcostal window"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "A small counterclockwise rotation at the apex enlarges the right ventricle in the image; the plane stays through the centre of the left ventricle.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Apical five-chamber",
            markdown: r#"
From the four-chamber view, **tilt the beam anteriorly** until the left ventricular outflow tract,
the aortic valve and the start of the ascending aorta appear (Mitchell et al., p26). A posterior tilt
would instead show the coronary sinus.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Apical)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("a5c")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "a5c")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which tilt brings the aortic valve into the apical view?",
                choices: ["Anterior", "Posterior", "Toward the patient's left hip", "No tilt, only depth"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The aortic root lies anterior to the mitral valve. Tilting anteriorly from the four-chamber view reaches it; a posterior tilt shows the coronary sinus.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Apical two-chamber",
            markdown: r#"
From the four-chamber view **rotate about 60 degrees counterclockwise** (Mitchell et al., p30). You
see the left atrium, mitral valve and left ventricle, and **no right ventricle**. Only the left
heart lies in this plane.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Apical)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("a2c")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "a2c")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which chamber is absent from the apical two-chamber view?",
                choices: ["The right ventricle", "The left atrium", "The left ventricle", "The mitral valve"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The two-chamber plane passes through the left atrium, mitral valve and left ventricle; the right ventricle lies outside it.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Apical three-chamber",
            markdown: r#"
Rotate a **further 60 degrees counterclockwise** (Mitchell et al., p30). The plane now holds the
left atrium, the mitral valve, the left ventricle, the **aortic valve and the aorta**: the apical
long axis.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Apical)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("a3c")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "a3c")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "What does the three-chamber view add to the two-chamber view?",
                choices: ["The aortic valve and the aorta", "The tricuspid valve", "The right atrium", "The inferior vena cava"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Turning a further 60 degrees brings the left ventricular outflow tract and aortic valve into the plane.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Subcostal four-chamber",
            markdown: r#"
With the patient **supine**, place the transducer under the xiphoid process with the marker toward
the patient's **left** (3 o'clock) and aim at the left shoulder (Mitchell et al., p31). The
ultrasound beam is perpendicular to the septa, which makes this the view for septal defects and the
thickness of the right ventricular wall. Held inspiration helps in a patient; in this model the
breath is not needed.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Subcostal)),
                Ui(SetEchoView("sc_4c")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            view_check: Some(ViewCheck(view: "sc_4c")),
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Why is the subcostal four-chamber view useful for the septa?",
                choices: ["The beam is perpendicular to the septa", "The septa are parallel to the beam", "The lungs are out of the way", "It shows the apex best"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "From below, the beam crosses the interatrial and interventricular septa at right angles, so defects and wall thickness are seen clearly.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Views this model cannot show",
            markdown: r#"
Two views of the guideline are **not reachable in this model**:

* the **subcostal inferior vena cava**: the anatomy has only a short stub of the vein, not the several
  centimetres the view needs;
* the **suprasternal arch**: the arch is in the model, but the best plane scores too low to count as
  found.

The Echo page lists each with the reason. The patient here is supine; the guideline images the parasternal and apical
windows in the left lateral position.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(ProbeAtWindow(Parasternal)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "A view is listed as not reachable in this model. What does that mean?",
                choices: ["The anatomy cannot be imaged that way within the probe's limits", "The view has no clinical use", "The probe is broken", "The view needs a different scenario"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "It is a statement about the model, not about patients: with this anatomy, supine, no pose reaches the target within the transducer's contact limit.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Measuring: M-mode and the LV diameters",
            markdown: r#"
**M-mode** records one scan line over time: depth runs down, time runs to the right, and the ECG
lead under it is on the same clock. Open the **Measure** section, choose **M-mode** and drag the
yellow cursor on the sector (the button *Mitral tips* aims it for you). In the parasternal long axis
the guideline puts the cursor perpendicular to the long axis of the ventricle, **just beyond the
mitral leaflet tips** (Mitchell et al., p31).

* **LVIDd**, the internal diameter in diastole, is read at the first frame after the mitral valve
  closes (the peak of the R wave when the valve cannot be seen); **LVIDs** at the smallest cavity.
* Freeze the trace, put the depth caliper on the septal and the posterior endocardium, name it, add
  it to the list: the model then shows the value it holds for that line and instant, and your
  difference.

Sweep speed: 100 mm/s is the usual speed for measuring times (p18). This is a measurement of a
model, to learn the method; no normal range is given here.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(EchoMeasure("lvid")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "At which moment of the cycle is the end-diastolic dimension read?",
                choices: ["The first frame after the mitral valve closes", "The peak of the T wave", "The moment the aortic valve opens", "The smallest cavity"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "End-diastole is the first frame after mitral leaflet closure (the R wave when the valve is not seen). The smallest cavity is the end-systolic dimension.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Measuring: TAPSE on an apical M-mode",
            markdown: r#"
**TAPSE** is the distance the **leading edge of the lateral tricuspid annulus** moves from end-diastole
toward the apex at end-systole. The M-mode cursor is put along the right ventricular free wall, as
parallel as possible to the movement of the annulus (Mitchell et al., p33). The button *Tricuspid
annulus* aims it from the apical four-chamber view.

A cursor that is not parallel to the motion sees only part of it: the model tells you the excursion
along your beam and, beside it, its own reading for the beat along the annulus's own axis and the
angle between the two.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(EchoMeasure("tapse")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "The M-mode cursor makes 30 degrees with the motion of the annulus. What happens to the excursion you read?",
                choices: ["It is smaller than the true excursion", "It is larger than the true excursion", "It is unchanged", "It becomes negative"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "You see only the part of the motion along the beam: the true excursion times the cosine of the angle.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Measuring: tissue Doppler at the annulus",
            markdown: r#"
**Tissue Doppler** records the velocity of the annulus itself, not of blood: the velocity of one point
of the mitral or tricuspid annulus along the beam, positive toward the probe. Click an annulus in the
sector (or use the buttons) to place the gate. The waves are named **S'** (systole, toward the apex),
**e'** (early diastole) and **a'** (atrial contraction, absent without one); the guideline sets the
velocity scale at 25 cm/s or lower and the sweep at 100 mm/s (Mitchell et al., p44).

The velocity is **not corrected for the angle** between the beam and the motion: the angle is shown, and
at 60 degrees the peak you read is half the true one. The thin band round the curve is drawn for the
look and is labelled designed. There is no blood Doppler in this simulator.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Echo)),
                Ui(EchoMeasure("e-prime")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which of the three waves is absent in atrial fibrillation?",
                choices: ["The atrial wave (a')", "The systolic wave (S')", "The early diastolic wave (e')", "None of them"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "a' follows the atrial contraction; with no organised atrial activity there is none.",
            )),
        ),
    ],
)
